# {{.AppName}} (eletrocromo Android)

Generated ad-hoc Android host for package **`{{.PackageID}}`**.

The Go library runs inside the activity process. The WebView asks that
library for each page.

## Layout

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `eletrocromo.json` | Generator config (re-run / rebuild inputs) |
| `app/` | Activity, in-process WebView, and the Go library |
| `app/src/main/jniLibs/<abi>/libeletrocromo.so` | Cross-compiled Go app (`ELETROCROMO_NO_UI=1`) |

## Prerequisites

- JDK 17+
- Android SDK (platform 35) + build-tools; `ANDROID_HOME` set
- Go toolchain that can `GOOS=android` with `CGO_ENABLED=0`
- Gradle 8.9+ (or open in Android Studio to bootstrap the wrapper)

## Go libraries

`lewkit release build --goos android --app` builds the libraries on one workflow.

`jni` depends on every ABI step, and it is the default.
`abi/arm64-v8a` depends on `toolexec` when the build is cgo-free.
`toolexec` compiles `_cgo_android_export.go` in, which publishes `JNI_OnLoad`.
`abi/armeabi-v7a` and `abi/x86_64` do not use that wrapper, so they build beside it.
Those steps call `gocmd`. A cgo-free `arm64-v8a` library is in the embedded
compiler's scope, so `--go lewkit` builds it. cgo and the other ABIs use the
host `go` command.

`eletrocromo.json` lists the ABIs. The default is `arm64-v8a`.

Your Go `main` should call `eletrocromo.App.Run()` with
`ID: "{{.PackageID}}"`. A cgo build passes `--cgo` so the activity can load
`Java_lewkit_Host_start`.

## Assemble APK

```bash
# if no gradlew yet:
gradle wrapper
./gradlew assembleDebug
# APK: app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
```

## Regenerate

From the eletrocromo repo:

```bash
go run ./cmd/eletrocromo android create \
  --id {{.PackageID}} \
  --name {{.AppName | printf "%q"}} \
  --out <this-dir> \
  --force
```

Do not hand-edit generated Java if you expect to re-run `create --force`;
put app logic in the Go module instead.
